`$` is the general directive marker. This page owns template-specific directive
semantics; general ownership, context, argument and diagnostic rules live in
@../directives (Directives).

The template directives documented here are current supported semantics. General
non-template directives such as `$config`, `$project`, `$html_builder` and `$page`
are accepted queued design and have their own signature owners.

Directive availability comes from one merged registry:

- frontend directives are always available
- a project builder may add constrained project-specific template directives
- an unknown directive is a syntax or rule error

### Frontend directives

`$before = value` and `$after = value`:

- add a prefix and suffix to each direct child; either directive may be omitted
- each may appear once per head and accepts a compile-time String or constant template
- values may contain named slots, but not default or positional slots; child insertion is implicit
- produce no wrapper for structurally absent output, but wrap a selected empty String
- wrap a direct child loop's aggregate output once

`$fresh`:

- opts one child out of the immediate parent's before/after wrapper

`$slot`:

- declares a default, named or positional slot

`$in name`:

- contributes content to a named slot

`$note` and `$todo`:

- discard their balanced bodies
- are template-authored comments

`$doc`:

- marks a documentation-comment template surface

`$raw`:

- preserves authored body content and whitespace without a formatter

`$md`:

- applies Moth Markdown formatting

### HTML builder directives

The selected HTML project builder may add constrained template-formatting directives:

`$html`:

- treats the body as raw HTML content

`$css`:

- applies the HTML builder's CSS checking and formatting path

`$escape_html`:

- escapes HTML-sensitive body content

`$code(language)`:

- highlights a code body
- removes formatting-only indentation from multiline bodies using the first content line as the baseline
- preserves indentation relative to that baseline
- escapes HTML-sensitive characters in `text` mode without applying syntax classes
- suppresses nested child-template interpretation inside the code content


Builder template directives remain constrained to formatting and composition contracts;
they cannot rewrite functions, change nominal identity, mutate HIR or weaken language
safety. For the shared directive family and no-empty-parentheses rule, see
@../directives (Directives).

### Formatter scope

Formatting directives do not flow automatically into nested child templates.

A nested child that needs `$md`, `$raw`, `$html`, `$css`, `$escape_html` or
`$code` declares that formatter itself.

Moth template files are the exception:

- a nested template with no explicit directive defaults to `$md`
- any explicit directive overrides that default

### Combining items in one head

A template head can hold several comma-separated items, such as an expression
and a formatter. Most directives combine freely with other items, but a few
change what the whole template is, so they need the head to themselves.

`$slot`, `$note`, `$todo` and `$doc` must be the only meaningful item in their
head. A slot declaration marks a place for content that arrives from elsewhere,
and the comment directives turn the template into a comment, so any other item
in that head would have nothing to act on:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    -- Error: the slot shares its head with a string
    card = [:
        <section>["prefix", $slot]</section>
    ]
]

Put the extra content in the surrounding template body instead:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    card = [:
        <section>prefix[$slot]</section>
    ]
]

Each template head can contain at most one formatter. `$md`, `$raw`, `$html`,
`$css`, `$escape_html` and `$code` each decide how the body is formatted, so
the compiler rejects a second formatter, including a repeat of the same one:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    -- Error: both directives format the body
    io.line([$md, $raw: Hello])
]

`$in name` can sit beside other items, but it can appear only once per
head. Each insert sends its body to one named slot, so use one insert template
for each slot you fill:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    [card:
        [$in title: Hello]
        [$in footer: Goodbye]
        Body content
    ]
]
